@meta
id: 25-10
title: The search you already did
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-pattern
par: 24
minutes: 5
vscode: find the word, look, scroll to the next one, select the gap, delete
open: incident.log

@brief
An on-call paste with two gateway failures in it. The first one retried
and settled — noise. The second one is the incident. Trim the noise: from
the first `ERROR` line down to the line just above the second `ERROR`.

You will search for `ERROR` to find the top of that block anyway. Having
searched, you should not have to type the pattern a second time: `\/` is
an address meaning "the next line matching the search I last used", and
`\?` is the same backwards. They read the same register the `n` key does.

@keys
| `\/` | as an address: next match of the last search pattern |
| `\?` | previous match of the last search pattern |
| `:.,\/-1d` | from here to one line above the next match |
| `\&` | next match of the last *substitute* pattern |

@hints
Search for `ERROR` first. That puts you on the top line of the block and
loads the pattern.
--
The bottom of the block is one line above the next match, which is
`\/-1`.
--
`/ERROR` Enter, then `:.,\/-1d` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    /ERROR<Enter>  :.,\/-1d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty keystrokes for a block whose length you never counted.

`\/` matters more than it looks. Retyping a pattern is not just extra
keys — it is a second chance to type it differently from the first, and
an address that quietly matches the wrong line is a bad way to find out.
Searching once and referring to that search keeps the two ends honest.

It also composes with everything else in the chapter:

    :\?,\/d              from the previous match to the next one
    :.,\/-1>             indent to just above the next match
    :\/+1,$d             everything below the next match

`n` and `N` share the same pattern, so a workflow of "search, look at
one hit, then act from the cursor to the following hit" never needs the
pattern typed twice. And `\&` reaches for the last `:s` pattern instead,
which is different from the search pattern more often than you expect.

